### Bad Code Snippet (Vulnerable)

This example demonstrates a **vulnerable** use of `URL` and `URLConnection` where user-supplied input is directly used to construct a request, which can lead to Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) if not properly validated.

```java
import java.net.URL;
import java.net.URLConnection;

public class VulnerableCode {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Assume this URL comes from an untrusted user input (e.g., a web form parameter)
        String userInputUrl = "http://localhost:8080/admin"; 

        try {
            // Directly using user input to create a URL without proper sanitization or validation.
            URL url = new URL(userInputUrl);

            // Attempting to connect to the remote resource.
            URLConnection connection = url.openConnection();

            System.out.println("Attempting to connect to: " + url);

            // In a real application, this connection might be used to fetch sensitive data.
            // This is vulnerable because an attacker could supply internal IPs or malicious protocols.
            connection.connect(); 

        } catch (java.net.MalformedURLException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: Invalid URL format.");
        } catch (java.net.UnknownHostException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: Unknown host.");
        } catch (java.net.ConnectException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: Connection refused. (This might indicate an internal network scan attempt.)");
        }
    }
}
```

***

### Good Code Snippet (Fixed)

The fix involves implementing strict validation on the input URL to ensure it only points to expected, safe domains or protocols, preventing SSRF attacks.

```java
import java.net.URL;
import java.net.URLConnection;
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.URISyntaxException;

public class FixedCode {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Assume this URL comes from an untrusted user input.
        String userInputUrl = "http://localhost:8080/admin"; 

        // --- FIX: Input Validation and Sanitization ---
        // 1. Use URI class for robust parsing and validation.
        URI uri;
        try {
            uri = new URI(userInputUrl);
        } catch (URISyntaxException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: Invalid URI syntax provided.");
            return;
        }

        // 2. Implement strict validation: Only allow HTTP/HTTPS protocols and specific host patterns.
        String scheme = uri.getScheme();
        if (!"http".equalsIgnoreCase(scheme) && !"https".equalsIgnoreCase(scheme)) {
            System.err.println("Security Error: Only HTTP and HTTPS protocols are allowed.");
            return;
        }

        // Further validation could involve checking the host against a whitelist of allowed domains.
        String host = uri.getHost();
        if (host != null && !host.startsWith("http://localhost") && !host.startsWith("https://api.trusted.com")) {
             System.err.println("Security Error: Host is not in the allowed whitelist.");
             return;
        }
        // ---------------------------------------------


        try {
            // Only proceed if validation passes.
            URL url = uri.toURL();

            // Attempting to connect to the remote resource.
            URLConnection connection = url.openConnection();

            System.out.println("Successfully validated and attempting to connect to: " + url);
            connection.connect(); 

        } catch (java.net.MalformedURLException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: URL conversion failed after validation.");
        } catch (java.net.ConnectException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: Connection refused.");
        }
    }
}
